   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in relation to scaffolding permit renewals",
    "basePrintNo": "S5369",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5369-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §3314.20, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "New York City Administrative Code",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5369                 Revised 06/17/15\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in\nrelation to scaffolding permit renewals\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo prevent building scaffolding in NYCHA developments from becoming a\npermanent fixture, disconnected from realistic construction schedules.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nNo scaffolding permit shall be renewed by the Commissioner of the\nDepartment of Buildings except for an \"ongoing construction project.\"\n\"Ongoing construction project\" is defined as a situation where persons\nemployed in construction work are utilizing such scaffolding at least\ntwo days a week, or have utilized such scaffolding for at least two\ndays per week at any time over the course of six months immediately\npreceding a request to renew a scaffolding permit.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAs of 2013, the New York City Housing Authority had 40.1 miles of\nsheds and scaffolds across their 334 developments in New York City.\nThese temporary scaffolds, which are frequently installed when\nbrickwork is being completed on the outside of the structures, have\nunfortunately become permanent problems. In many cases the scaffolding\nremains while construction has been halted, or after it has finished\ncompletely. At the Butler Houses in the Bronx, scaffolding remained in\nplace for five years, and dozens of other locations have had\nscaffolding in place for up to four years.\n\nThis permanent scaffolding has caused a multitude of public health and\nsafety issues. The scaffolding can serve as a collection site for\ntrash, has attracted rodents, and has been shown to fail in extreme\nweather situations. Beyond these issues, permanent scaffolding can\ndramatically impact the quality of life of the tenants in the\nbuildings, blocking sunlight and attracting drug and other illicit\nactivity. This bill seeks to reduce the amount of time these scaffolds\ncan sit idle, while also allowing for realistic construction and\nweather-related delays.\n\nFISCAL IMPACT:\n\nNone\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect on the thirtieth day after it shall become\nlaw\n\n",
